<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Centers of Mass</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose you have a beam whose length is <m>L</m> sitting on the
  <m>x</m>-axis so that <m>0\leq x \leq L</m>.  Suppose also that the density is
  constantly 1; that is, <m>\rho(x) = 1</m> grams per centimeter.
  Sketch the density function <m>\rho(x)</m>.  Then find the mass and
  center of mass of the beam.  Does your result make intuitive sense?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the mass and center of mass of a beam whose density is
  <m>\rho(x)=x^3</m> where <m>0\leq x\leq L</m>.  Sketch the density function and
  check that your result makes sense.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the mass and center of mass of a beam whose density is
  <m>\rho(x)=e^{-x}</m> where <m>0\leq x\leq 1</m>.  Sketch the density function and
  check that your result makes sense.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's now consider a 2-dimensional version of center of mass.  Suppose
that a plate in the triangular shape shown below on the left is made out of a
uniform material whose density is 2 grams per square centimeter.  We
would like to find the balancing point, which we call the center of
mass, of the plate.</p>
        <p><image source="figures/19-plate.eps"/> 
  <image source="figures/19-plateslice.eps"/></p>
        <p>Let's find the center of mass in the following way: first,
take a vertical slice as shown on the right above.  What is the area
<m>dA</m> of the 
slice taken at position <m>x</m> if the width is <m>dx</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the mass <m>dm</m> of this slice if the density is 2 grams per square
centimeter?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the total mass <m>M</m> of the plate?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is <m>\overline{x}</m>, the <m>x</m> coordinate of the center of mass?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is <m>\overline{y}</m>, the <m>y</m> coordinate of the center of mass?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that a cone is formed by spinning the region bounded by
  the <m>y</m>-axis, the line <m>y=x</m> and the line <m>y=4</m> about the <m>y</m>-axis.
  The units on the axes are in centimeters.  
  Suppose also that the material out of which the cone is made has a
  density of 3 g/cm<m>^3</m>.</p>
        <p>Suppose you slice the cone horiztonally to create a cylinder of
  height <m>dy</m>.  What is the volume <m>dV</m> of this slice?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the mass <m>dm</m> of this slice?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the total mass of the cone?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the <m>y</m> coordinate of the center of mass?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose now that the cone is filled with water contained a dissolved
  substance so that the density is <m>\rho(y) = 5-y</m> g/cm<m>^3</m>.  Find the
  <m>y</m> coordinate of the center of mass.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
